# Chapter 8 · Beyond the Syllabus Schools: Intersectional, Black, Postcolonial, Eco- and Muslim Feminism

> The syllabus lists six schools, but the examiner has moved on. The 2025 question asked for "a detailed analysis of the **intersectionality** of gender with other social identities". The 2024–2026 MCQs asked about **Black feminism**, **standpoint theory**, **strategic essentialism**, **kyriarchy**, the **matrix of domination**, the **patriarchal bargain** and **gynocriticism**. This chapter teaches all of them.

## 8.1  Black feminism

In the United States, Black women found that the women's movement was led by white women, and the civil-rights movement by Black men. Neither spoke for them.

- **Sojourner Truth**, a formerly enslaved woman, spoke at a women's convention in **Akron, Ohio**, in ==1851==. The famous version of her speech, with the refrain "**Ain't I a woman?**", was published by Frances Gage in 1863; historians note that it differs from earlier reports. Its point is clear either way: the ideal of the delicate, protected "lady" never applied to Black women who laboured in the fields.
- The **Combahee River Collective Statement** (Boston, ==1977==) said that Black women face **"interlocking" oppressions** — racial, sexual, heterosexual and class — experienced **simultaneously**.
- **bell hooks** (*Ain't I a Woman: Black Women and Feminism*, ==1981==; *Feminist Theory: From Margin to Center*, ==1984==) criticised white feminism for treating middle-class white women's problems as every woman's problems.
- **Audre Lorde** ("The Master's Tools Will Never Dismantle the Master's House", ==1979==) warned feminists not to ignore difference among women.
- **Alice Walker** coined **womanism** (*In Search of Our Mothers' Gardens*, ==1983==) for Black women's feminism.

::: define Black feminism | 2025 MCQ
Feminism centred on the experience of Black women, who face **race, class and gender oppression together**. It differs from mainstream feminism by its focus on **all three** — the answer to the 2025 MCQ was "**all of these**".
:::

::: thinker Patricia Hill Collins | *Black Feminist Thought* (1990)
Collins described a **matrix of domination**: the way intersecting oppressions — race, class, gender, sexuality, nation — are **organised across society** at several levels (structures, institutions, culture and personal life). She also described Black women as "**outsiders within**" — for example, domestic workers inside white homes, who see the family from a position no one else has.
-> A 2026 MCQ asked who developed the matrix of domination: **Patricia Hill Collins**.
:::

## 8.2  Intersectionality

::: thinker Kimberlé Crenshaw | "Demarginalizing the Intersection of Race and Sex" (1989); "Mapping the Margins" (1991)
Crenshaw, an American law professor, coined **intersectionality**. She used the case of ***DeGraffenreid v. General Motors*** (==1976==): Black women laid off by General Motors sued for discrimination. The court said GM hired women (white women, in office jobs) and hired Black people (Black men, on the factory floor), so there was no sex or race discrimination — and refused to combine the two claims. Black women fell **between** the categories.
-> Her image: discrimination is like traffic at an **intersection**; a woman standing where two roads cross can be hit from either direction, or both.
:::

A 2024 MCQ asked where intersectionality originated. The answer: from discussions on **gender and race**.

::: define Intersectionality | the key idea of the 2025 question
The idea that a person's social identities — such as **gender, race, class, religion, ethnicity, caste, disability, age and sexuality** — **overlap and interact**, producing **unique experiences** of privilege and disadvantage that cannot be understood by looking at one identity at a time.
-> It is an **analytical tool**: it tells you to ask "which women?" before making any claim about "women".
:::

::: simple
Being a woman is one disadvantage. Being poor is another. But a poor woman does not simply face "two disadvantages added up". She faces a **different situation** — for example, she cannot afford private transport, so she depends on unsafe public buses where she is harassed. The overlap creates a problem neither identity creates alone.
:::

::: fig venn | Intersectionality: overlapping identities create a unique position.
@centre Poor, rural, minority woman: multiplied disadvantage
Gender: a woman in a patriarchal society
Class and place: poor and rural
Religion or ethnicity: a minority
:::

### Additive versus intersectional thinking

::: table | Two ways of thinking about multiple disadvantage.
@widths 3.6,5.2,5.2
Aspect | Additive ("single-axis") | Intersectional
Model | Gender + class + religion, added up | Gender × class × religion, interacting
Example | "She faces sexism and also poverty." | "Her poverty changes what sexism means for her: no safe transport, no lawyer, no shelter."
Policy result | One programme for women, another for the poor | Programmes designed for the specific group at the overlap
:::

### Intersectionality in Pakistan

- **Rural women** face distance from schools and clinics on top of gender norms; the literacy gap between urban and rural women is far larger than between urban women and men (Census ==2023==: female literacy about ==53%== nationally, much lower in rural areas).
- **Religious minority women** — Christian, Hindu, Sikh, Ahmadi — face additional risks, including reported cases of **forced conversion and marriage** of Hindu and Christian girls, especially in Sindh and Punjab.
- **Ethnic minorities** such as the **Hazara** community of Quetta have faced targeted violence; Hazara widows carry both community trauma and women's burdens.
- **Women with disabilities** face barriers of access, stigma and marriage prospects that other women do not.
- **Khawaja siras** are at the intersection of gender identity, poverty and social exclusion (Topic 1).
- **Class:** a professional woman in Islamabad may hire a domestic worker, who is herself a woman with no labour protection — the privilege of one woman rests on the disadvantage of another.

## 8.3  Standpoint theory

::: define Standpoint theory | 2026 MCQ
The view that **knowledge is socially situated**: what people can see and know depends on their **position in society**. Those at the bottom of a power structure can see things that those at the top miss, so starting research from women's lives produces **more complete** knowledge.
-> Roots: Hegel's master and slave, and Marx's view that the working class sees capitalism more clearly than the owners.
:::

- **Nancy Hartsock** ("The Feminist Standpoint", ==1983==) — a Marxist-feminist version.
- **Dorothy Smith** (*The Everyday World as Problematic*, ==1987==) — sociology should start from women's everyday lives.
- **Sandra Harding** (*The Science Question in Feminism*, ==1986==) — "**strong objectivity**": science becomes *more* objective when it includes the views of the marginalised.
- **Patricia Hill Collins** (==1990==) — Black women's standpoint.

## 8.4  Postcolonial feminism

**Postcolonial** means "after colonialism": it studies how colonial rule shaped, and still shapes, the ideas and lives of formerly colonised peoples.

::: thinker Chandra Talpade Mohanty | "Under Western Eyes: Feminist Scholarship and Colonial Discourses" (1984; revised 1986)
Mohanty criticised Western feminist writing for creating a single "**Third World Woman**" — poor, ignorant, veiled, tradition-bound and a victim — set against the educated, free Western woman. This erases the real variety of women's lives and their own struggles.
-> The key text of postcolonial feminism.
:::

::: thinker Gayatri Chakravorty Spivak | "Can the Subaltern Speak?" (1988)
The **subaltern** is the person at the very bottom of colonial society, without a voice in the official record. Writing on the British ban on *sati*, Spivak summed up the colonial attitude as "**White men are saving brown women from brown men**". Both the colonisers and local patriarchs spoke *about* the women; the women themselves were not heard.
-> Also the source of **strategic essentialism** (Chapter 7).
:::

- **Lila Abu-Lughod** ("Do Muslim Women Really Need Saving?", ==2002==) criticised the use of Afghan women's rights to justify the ==2001== war, and the idea that Muslim women need rescue by the West.
- **Uma Narayan** (*Dislocating Cultures*, ==1997==) warned against treating "culture" as fixed, both in Western views of the East and in local claims about "our traditions".

::: pakistan | Postcolonial feminism and Pakistan
- It answers the charge that feminism is a "Western agenda": postcolonial feminists are themselves **critics of Western feminism**, and argue for feminisms rooted in local history.
- It also warns against **the other extreme** — defending every local custom as "our culture", when many harmful laws (such as parts of colonial penal law) and customs have changed over time.
- It shows how colonial law shaped women's lives in the subcontinent — for example, through the codification of personal law and the **Penal Code of 1860**.
:::

## 8.5  Ecofeminism

::: define Ecofeminism | women and nature
The view that the **domination of women** and the **domination of nature** are connected: both come from the same patriarchal, profit-driven way of thinking that ranks culture over nature, reason over emotion, and man over woman.
-> The word was coined by the French writer **Françoise d'Eaubonne** in *Le Féminisme ou la Mort* ("Feminism or Death", ==1974==).
:::

- **Carolyn Merchant** (*The Death of Nature*, ==1980==) argued that the scientific revolution turned nature from a living "mother" into a dead machine to be exploited.
- **Vandana Shiva** (*Staying Alive*, ==1988==), an Indian scientist, argued that colonial and Western "development" destroyed forests, rivers and women's knowledge together. With **Maria Mies** she wrote *Ecofeminism* (==1993==).
- The **Chipko movement** (Uttarakhand, India, from ==1973==), in which village women hugged trees to stop logging, became an ecofeminist symbol.
- **Wangari Maathai** founded the **Green Belt Movement** in Kenya (==1977==), in which women planted millions of trees; she won the Nobel Peace Prize in ==2004==.

::: balance | Ecofeminism and its critics
Some ecofeminists said women are **naturally** closer to nature — an essentialist claim. The Indian economist **Bina Agarwal** ("The Gender and Environment Debate", ==1992==) proposed **feminist environmentalism** instead: women's closeness to nature comes from their **material work** — collecting water, fuel and fodder — not from their biology.
-> In Pakistan this is the stronger argument: rural women in Thar, Cholistan and flood-hit Sindh depend daily on water and land, which is why climate disasters such as the ==2022== floods hit them hardest (Topic 4 and the 2026 question on climate).
:::

## 8.6  Muslim (Islamic) feminism

::: define Islamic (Muslim) feminism | gender justice from within Islam
A movement of scholars and activists who seek **gender justice** by **re-reading the Qur'an and Sunnah** — separating the divine message from later **patriarchal interpretation** (*tafsir*) and custom. It argues that the Qur'an's core values of **justice** (*adl*), **equality in faith** and **human dignity** support women's rights.
-> The term spread in the ==1990s==, partly through the Iranian magazine *Zanan* (==1992==) and the historian **Margot Badran**.
:::

::: table | Key thinkers of Muslim feminism.
@widths 4,2,8
Thinker | Work | Main idea
Fatima Mernissi (Morocco) | *Beyond the Veil* (==1975==); *The Veil and the Male Elite* (==1987==) | Questioned the chains of transmission of hadith used against women
Amina Wadud (USA) | *Qur'an and Woman* (==1992==) | Read the Qur'an from a woman's perspective; stressed equal creation and moral agency
Leila Ahmed (Egypt/USA) | *Women and Gender in Islam* (==1992==) | Traced how women's status changed in Muslim history
Riffat Hassan (Pakistan/USA) | Essays from the 1980s | Showed that the Qur'an does not say woman was created from man's rib
Asma Barlas (Pakistan/USA) | *"Believing Women" in Islam* (==2002==) | Argued the Qur'an is anti-patriarchal when read as a whole
:::

- Organisations: **Sisters in Islam** (Malaysia, ==1988==) and **Musawah** ("equality"), a global movement for equality in Muslim family law launched in Kuala Lumpur in ==2009==.

::: debate | Is "Islamic feminism" a bridge or a contradiction?
- **A bridge:** it speaks the language of faith that most Pakistani women share; it has legitimacy that secular feminism lacks; many rights — **inheritance**, ***mahr***, ***khula***, consent to marriage — are already Islamic.
- **A contradiction (secular critics):** some secular feminists, such as **Haideh Moghissi** (==1999==), argue that working inside religion limits how far reform can go. In Pakistan, **Afiya Zia** (*Faith and Feminism in Pakistan*, ==2018==) criticised the turn to faith-based approaches as limiting secular feminist politics.
- **A Western agenda (conservative critics):** some religious conservatives say it distorts Islam to suit Western ideas.
- **Beyond resistance:** the anthropologist **Saba Mahmood** (*Politics of Piety*, ==2005==) studied women's mosque movements in Egypt and argued that pious women exercise real **agency**, even when they do not seek "liberation" in the Western sense.
-> A balanced answer: in Pakistan, the most successful reforms have combined constitutional rights with Islamic justification — for example, the Council of Islamic Ideology's debates and the courts' support for *khula* and inheritance.
:::

## 8.7  Five more terms the MCQs love

::: table | Five concepts from recent MCQs.
@widths 3.2,3.8,7
Term | Thinker | Meaning
Patriarchal bargain | **Deniz Kandiyoti**, "Bargaining with Patriarchy" (==1988==) | The **strategies women adopt** to secure security and power within the limits of a given patriarchy. In "classic patriarchy" (from North Africa across the Middle East to South Asia, including Pakistan), a young bride is powerless, but she can expect power later as a **mother of sons and mother-in-law** — so women may invest in the system rather than resist it (MCQs 2026).
Kyriarchy | **Elisabeth Schüssler Fiorenza**, *But She Said* (==1992==) | From the Greek *kyrios*, "lord": **interlocking systems of domination** — gender, race, class, empire — in which a person may be oppressed in one and privileged in another (MCQ 2026).
Matrix of domination | **Patricia Hill Collins** (==1990==) | How intersecting oppressions are organised across society (MCQ 2026).
Gynocriticism | **Elaine Showalter** ("Toward a Feminist Poetics", ==1979==; *A Literature of Their Own*, ==1977==) | The study of **women as writers** — their history, themes and traditions (MCQ 2024).
Écriture féminine | **Hélène Cixous** (==1975==) | "Women's writing" from the female body and experience (MCQ 2024).
:::

::: fig tree | The newer feminisms at a glance.
Beyond the six schools
- Black feminism: race, class and gender together
  - Truth
  - Combahee
  - hooks
- Intersectionality: overlapping identities
  - Crenshaw
  - Collins
- Standpoint: knowledge is situated
  - Hartsock
  - Harding
- Postcolonial: against speaking for "Third World women"
  - Mohanty
  - Spivak
- Ecofeminism: women and nature
  - d'Eaubonne
  - Shiva
- Muslim feminism: justice from within Islam
  - Mernissi
  - Wadud
  - Barlas
:::
